<h2 id="texas-taxes">Texas taxes: what "no income tax" really means</h2>
<p>Texas does not tax personal income, so every dollar of side hustle profit escapes a state income tax bite that would cost you 4–10% in many other states. That is a real advantage, but it is not the whole picture:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Federal income tax</strong> applies to all side hustle profit at your normal bracket.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax</strong> of 15.3% (Social Security and Medicare) applies once you net $400 or more in a year. For 2026 the Social Security portion covers the first $184,500 of combined wages and self-employment earnings.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax</strong> may apply. If you sell physical products, or certain services Texas treats as taxable (some lawn care, landscaping and data processing services, for example), you need a free sales and use tax permit from the Texas Comptroller and must collect the state's 6.25% rate plus any local tax, up to 8.25% total.</li>
  <li><strong>Property taxes are high</strong> in Texas, which is one reason so many residents look for extra income in the first place.</li>
</ul>
<p>A simple rule: set aside 20–30% of side hustle profit for federal taxes. The estimator below shows what that looks like for your income, and you can log mileage in a [[amazon:mileage-log|mileage log book]] to cut what you owe.</p>

[[tool:tax-estimator]]

<h2 id="trends">Side hustle trends driving demand in Texas</h2>

<div class="hustle-grid">
