<h2 id="getting-started">How to start a side hustle in Texas</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Pick a hustle that fits your city and hours.</strong> If you are unsure, take our <a href="/tools/side-hustle-quiz/">side hustle quiz</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Check licensing and permits.</strong> Trades like electrical, plumbing and HVAC require state licensing; cities may require permits for home businesses, food or short-term rentals.</li>
  <li><strong>Register for sales tax if needed.</strong> Selling products or taxable services requires a free permit from the Texas Comptroller.</li>
  <li><strong>Consider a DBA or LLC.</strong> A DBA (assumed name certificate) is filed with your county clerk if you operate under a business name. An LLC files with the Texas Secretary of State and has annual franchise tax reporting, though small businesses under the no-tax-due threshold typically owe no franchise tax.</li>
  <li><strong>Track mileage and expenses from day one.</strong> In a driving-heavy state, mileage is often your biggest deduction.</li>
  <li><strong>Know your true hourly rate.</strong> Run driving and delivery numbers through a gig earnings calculator (there is one in the sidebar) before committing to a platform.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> Summer heat is a real safety issue for delivery, yard and event work. Carry water, schedule outdoor jobs early, and price summer work accordingly.</div>

<h2 id="mistakes">Mistakes Texas side hustlers make</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Assuming "no income tax" means no tax.</strong> Federal income tax and self-employment tax still take a meaningful share of profit. Make quarterly estimated payments once you expect to owe $1,000 or more for the year.</li>
  <li><strong>Ignoring vehicle costs.</strong> Texas metros are spread out, and long deadhead miles back from the suburbs or the airport quietly cut your hourly rate. Track every mile.</li>
  <li><strong>Skipping insurance.</strong> Personal auto policies often exclude gig driving unless you add rideshare coverage, and home-service work calls for basic general liability insurance before you work on someone else's property.</li>
  <li><strong>Collecting no sales tax on taxable sales.</strong> Craft fair, Etsy and pop-up sellers sometimes forget that Texas expects a sales tax permit. Marketplaces collect it on most online orders, but in-person sales are on you.</li>
  <li><strong>Underpricing in expensive weather.</strong> Summer outdoor work and post-storm jobs are harder and in higher demand. Price them that way.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="bottom-line">The bottom line</h2>
<p>Texas gives side hustlers a rare combination: big, growing markets, a busy event calendar and no state income tax. Start with what your city is best at, whether that is airport rideshare in Dallas, event work in Austin or storm-ready home services in Houston, and add a skill-based hustle over time. Better Side Hustles keeps adding Texas city guides, so check back for more local ideas.</p>
